==Latin==

===Etymology===
{{etymon|la|id=the Americas|:der|it:Amerigo<id:personal name>}}
Feminine form of ''Americus'', the Latinized form of the forename of Italian explorer [[:w:Amerigo Vespucci|Amerigo Vespucci]] (1454–1512).  {{m|it|Amerigo}} is the {{der|la|it|-}} form of a {{der|la|gem|-}} personal name (see {{m|en|Emmerich}}).

First recorded in 1507 (together with the related term ''[[Amerige|Amerigen]]'') in the ''{{w|Cosmographiae Introductio}}'', apparently written by [[w:Matthias Ringmann|Matthias Ringmann]], in reference to South America;<ref>John R. Hebert, "The Map That Named America: Library Acquires 1507 Waldseemüller Map of the World" ([http://www.loc.gov/loc/lcib/0309/maps.html]), ''Information Bulletin'', Library of Congress</ref> first applied to both North and South America by Mercator in 1538. ''Amerigen'' means "land of Amerigo" and derives from ''Amerigo'' and ''[[gen]]'', the accusative case of Greek ''[[γῆ|gē]]'' "earth". ''America'' accorded with the feminine names of [[Asia]], [[Africa]], and [[Europa]].<ref name=Smiths>Toby Lester, "Putting America on the Map", ''Smithsonian'', 40:9 (December 2009)</ref>

===Pronunciation===
* {{la-IPA|Amerīca|eccl=no}}
* {{la-IPA|America|classical=no}}
* Note: the length of the /i/ should be long, and that form is well-attested in Latin literature; for example, {{w|Rafael Landívar|Rafael Landívar's|lang=es}} Rusticatio Mexicana. This form is mostly attested in poetry, and it corresponds more closely to the Italian pronunciation of {{m|it|Amerigo}}.<ref>{{cite-journal|es|author=Juan Gil|title=America|journal=Myrtia|volume=25|year=2010|pages=187-194|issn=0213-7674|url=https://revistas.um.es/myrtia/article/view/131651}}</ref> Nevertheless, in Ecclesiastical Latin, due to analogy with Italian {{l|it|America}}, the stress is instead shifted to the third-to-last syllable, implying the /i/ to be short.

